Below is a step‐by‐step breakdown and the corresponding goal model tables that decompose the mission “Deliver Food to Inpatient Room” into a hierarchical structure of goals and tasks. The design addresses the two alternative food delivery methods (direct delivery versus assisted retrieval), the required meal tracking, and the dish retrieval with its own alternatives (including door opening coordination).

─────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Top‐Level Goal  
─────────────────────────────  
• G1: Deliver Food to Inpatient Room  
  – This goal represents the overall mission: delivering food from the kitchen to the inpatient room based on an order-by-order request.  
  – Because the food can be delivered either directly (by depositing food on the table) or by assisted retrieval (where the inpatient or another agent fetches food from the tray), these two methods form an OR alternative. In addition, the mission requires that meal retrieval details be tracked and that dirty dishes be retrieved. Therefore, the runtime structure for G1 combines a fallback (for delivery alternatives) and sequential execution for the additional mandatory actions.  

Runtime for G1: (FALLBACK(G2, G3); G5; G6)  
– Explanation:  
 • FALLBACK(G2, G3) means that either direct delivery (G2) or assisted retrieval (G3) will satisfy the delivery requirement.  
 • G5 (Meal Retrieval Tracking) and G6 (Retrieve Dirty Dishes) must be performed sequentially (AND) regardless of which delivery method is chosen.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 2. Direct Delivery vs. Assisted Retrieval  
─────────────────────────────  
• G2: Direct Food Delivery  
  – This perform goal has the robot physically deposit food on the patient’s room table using its special manipulation skills.  
  – It is a leaf goal (no further decomposition) because the delivery is direct.

• G3: Assisted Food Retrieval  
  – This perform goal enables the inpatient (or companion/nurse/another robot) to retrieve food from the robot’s tray.  
  – It first requires checking if retrieval is possible via a query (G3.1), then signaling which meal to retrieve, waiting for the action, and finally alerting if the wrong meal is fetched.
  – Runtime for G3 is sequential: (G3.1; AT3; AT4; AT5)

• G3.1: Query Patient Retrieval Capability  
  – This query goal obtains information from the inpatient record regarding whether the designated person (inpatient, companion, or nurse) can retrieve food from the tray.  
  – It is a leaf goal with a runtime annotation of “–”.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 3. Additional Mandatory Goals  
─────────────────────────────  
• G5: Meal Retrieval Tracking  
  – This perform goal requires that the robot log the details of the meal retrieval (which meal, when, and where) and alert if an incorrect meal is retrieved.  
  – It is a leaf goal.

• G6: Retrieve Dirty Dishes  
  – This perform goal specifies that dirty dishes must be retrieved from the room.  
  – Because dish retrieval can be done unassisted, cooperatively with another robot, or with human assistance (door opening), we further decompose G6 into three alternatives:
   – G6.1: Door Opening Assistance  
   – G6.2: Unassisted Dish Retrieval  
   – G6.3: Cooperative Dish Retrieval  
  – The runtime for G6 is expressed as:  
   FALLBACK(G6.1, FALLBACK(G6.2, G6.3))  
   (Any one of these alternatives suffices.)

• G6.1: Door Opening Assistance  
  – This perform goal coordinates with a human in the room to open the door when needed.  
  – It is a leaf goal.

• G6.2: Unassisted Dish Retrieval  
  – This perform goal has the robot retrieve dirty dishes by itself.  
  – It is a leaf goal.

• G6.3: Cooperative Dish Retrieval  
  – This perform goal has two robots cooperate to retrieve dirty dishes.  
  – It is a leaf goal.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 4. Task Decomposition  
─────────────────────────────  
Tasks are concrete actions executed by one or more robots. They are linked to the appropriate goals as follows:

• Under G2 (Direct Food Delivery):  
  – AT1: Navigate to Inpatient Room  
  – AT2: Deposit Food on Table

• Under G3 (Assisted Food Retrieval):  
  – AT3: Indicate Meal Retrieval  
  – AT4: Wait for Retrieval  
  – AT5: Alert on Wrong Meal Retrieval

• Under G5 (Meal Retrieval Tracking):  
  – AT6: Track Meal Retrieval

• Under G6.1 (Door Opening Assistance):  
  – AT8: Acknowledge Door Opening Signal

• Under G6.2 (Unassisted Dish Retrieval):  
  – AT10: Retrieve Dirty Dishes (Unassisted)

• Under G6.3 (Cooperative Dish Retrieval):  
  – AT11: Retrieve Dirty Dishes (Cooperative)

